## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to architect a complete, safety-critical computer vision pipeline operating under real-time constraints in dynamic, cluttered environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular 3D reconstruction leveraging temporal and geometric cues
- Multi-object tracking in scenarios with frequent clutter and occlusion
- Hazard prediction from partial observations and contact-event inference
- Few-shot on-device adaptation for novel object categories
- Edge-GPU optimization: latency, memory footprint, and compute budget trade-offs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: modules and data flow
class Monocular3DSystem {
  constructor() {
    this.depthNet = new LightweightDepthEncoder();
    this.tracker = new MultiObjectTracker();
    this.hazardPredictor = new ContactEventPredictor();
    this.adaptationHead = new FewShotAdapter();
  }

  processFrame(frame, context = {}) {
    const depth = this.depthNet.infer(frame);
    const poses = this.depthNet.extractEgoMotion(depth, context.prevDepth);
    const objects = this.tracker.associate(depth, poses);
    const hazards = this.hazardPredictor.predict(objects, depth, poses);
    return { depth, poses, objects, hazards };
  }

  adapt(shots) {
    return this.adaptationHead.fineTune(shots);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a practical monocular 6-DoF pose estimator that handles symmetry, occlusion, and online adaptation in a real-world manufacturing setting. Emphasizes differentiable refinement, data strategies, and latency planning.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular 6-DoF pose estimation
- Differentiable rendering for pose refinement
- Symmetry handling and occlusion robustness
- Synthetic data + domain randomization
- Online adaptation with few-shot updates
- Real-time latency targets (embedded GPU)

## Code Example
```javascript
class PoseHead {
  constructor(featDim) {
    this.fcRot = linear(featDim, 4); // quaternion
    this.fcTrans = linear(featDim, 3);
  }
  forward(feat) {
    const q = normalize(this.fcRot(feat), 2);
    const t = this.fcTrans(feat);
    return { quat: q, trans: t };
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate symmetry handling for objects with known symmetry? 
- How would you mitigate catastrophic forgetting during online adaptation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical monocular depth reasoning and tracking in a simple inspection setting.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular depth proxy with known geometry
- Lightweight detectors, on-device adaptation
- Temporal tracking and data efficiency

## Code Example
```javascript
function projectTo3D(bbox, sheetDims, intrinsics) {
  // heuristic: depth proxy from vertical size and known sheet height
  const heightPx = bbox.height;
  const Z = (sheetDims.height * intrinsics.fy) / heightPx;
  return {X: 0, Y: 0, Z};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would occlusion or specular glare affect depth proxy? how to mitigate?
- How would you measure success and compare to a stereo baseline?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses the ability to architect privacy-aware computer vision systems under strict edge constraints while balancing accuracy and privacy guarantees. Requires concrete architecture, differentiable privacy terms, and rigorous evaluation plans.

## Key Concepts

- Privacy-preserving CV and de-identification modules
- Differentiable privacy loss and threat-model-aware evaluation
- Edge/on-device inference and latency budgeting
- Trade-offs between detection accuracy and privacy guarantees

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lightweight total loss combining detection accuracy and privacy
function computeTotalLoss(detectionPred, privacyPred, targets) {
    const detectionLoss = computeDetectionLoss(detectionPred, targets);
    const privacyLoss = computePrivacyLoss(privacyPred, targets.sensitiveRegions);

    // Weighted combination with privacy budget constraint
    return detectionLoss + lambdaPrivacy * privacyLoss;
}

// Differentiable de-identification module
class PrivacyModule {
    obfuscate(features, sensitiveRegions) {
        // Apply learnable obfuscation to sensitive regions
        return features.map((feat, i) => 
            sensitiveRegions[i] ? this.anonymize(feat) : feat
        );
    }
}
```
